            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_3ede44af">C1:002</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id323609f6" xml:lang="de">Eine Abhandlung von den musicalischen Intervallen und Geschlechten</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_cf78cfa1">
               <persName role="collaborator" xml:id="persName_71be34e1">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idfeef4062">
               <date isodate="1739" xml:id="date_f26bcf02">1739</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id480378ef">
               <p xml:id="idm107">As Scheibe notes in his last theoretical treatise, <rend xml:id="idm108" fontstyle="italic">Über die musikalische Composition</rend> (1773; SchW C1:011, p. 45), he was often in close contact with his friend <persName xml:id="idm109" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> while living in Hamburg. Besides helping Telemann on various occasions, composing for instance at least one cantata for Telemann while he was visiting Paris (SchW B2:045), Scheibe also sought guidance regarding the journal<rend xml:id="idm110" fontstyle="italic"> Der Critische Musicus</rend> (1738–40; SchW C1:008). They also discussed the subject of the present treatise which Scheibe had started on while still in Leipzig. In the foreword, he recounts that he became interested in the subject sometime during 1729–31, that is while he still received tuition in composition in Leipzig. He found the traditional approach insufficient and decided to study the matter in detail, including studying the musical scores of 'experienced masters and composers'. When he arrived in Hamburg, discussions with Telemann convinced Scheibe that his proposal of a new system was indeed appropriate which was supported by his friend. After its publication in 1739, according to which it was authored by Scheibe and produced 'at the expense of the author' (title page), Telemann proposed changes and they friendly disagreed on some of the calculations of the musical intervals. While Scheibe had found employment in Copenhagen, Telemann presented his ideas of the system at a meeting in the 'Societät der musikalischen Wissenschaften' in 1743. In 1750 Scheibe becomes aware of the meeting and mentions the treatise in a letter to Telemann: 'But I was a little surprised when I discovered that my entire system of intervals published in 1739 was set at your honour’s expenses. I hope that the same Musical Society has not been given the opportunity to think and write [on the system], for – as your honour is still well aware of – you have assured me that I was the first one to completely establish, present and make known this order of the intervals'. Telemann’s presentation and a critical response by <persName xml:id="idm111" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christoph Gottlieb Schröter</persName> was later published in <rend xml:id="idm112" fontstyle="italic">
                     <persName xml:id="idm113" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Lorenz Mizler</persName>s musikalische Bibliothek</rend> (1752) which provoked Scheibe who wrote to Telemann seeking an explanation. Telemann excused the incident. However, it led to the idea that they had collaborated on the study – which even Scheibe promoted in his <rend xml:id="idm114" fontstyle="italic">Abhandlung vom Ursprunge</rend> (1754; SchW C1:001) – and in a review of one of Scheibe’s other publications, it is mentioned that Scheibe also explains 'the Telemannian system of intervals, of which he was a co-inventor' ('von dem Telemannischen Intervallensystem, davon er Miterfinder ist.'). Later the work was revised as 'G.Ph. Telemann’s last work from his 86th year, consisting of a table of musical sounds and intervals' and published in <rend xml:id="idm115" fontstyle="italic">Unterhaltungen</rend> (Hamburg, 1767). Thus in 1773, Scheibe seeks to rectify the misconception that Telemann was the originator of the new approach to understanding the musical intervals. There is no doubt, however, that Telemann and Scheibe discussed the subject in detail already during the late 1730s when they both were in Hamburg though they did not necessarily agree on all the aspects. When Scheibe died in April 1776, they started cataloguing his library with the view of holding an auction later that year. <persName xml:id="idm116" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Giovanni Battista Martini</persName> in Bologna heard about Scheibe's death and wrote promptly to Kapellmeister <persName xml:id="idm117" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Paolo Scalabrini</persName> in Copenhagen, indicating those of Scheibe's books that he would be interested in obtaining. Together with the former director of The Royal Theatre, <persName xml:id="idm118" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Georg Nielsen</persName>, Scalabrini got hold of <rend xml:id="idm119" fontstyle="italic">Eine Abhandlung</rend> and gave it to the singer <persName xml:id="idm120" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Teresa Torre</persName>, who had just retired moving back to Lucca. She would then hand it over, together with another treatise (cf. SchW C1:008), to Padre Martini when she arrived in Italy.</p>
            </history>
